# UI / views layer

React-flavoured code lives in `src/components/`. Editor surface is `NodeEditor.tsx`. Each processor ships **two independent React components**: a graph-canvas card (`flow-nodes/views/<Name>NodeView.tsx`) and a settings-pane (`flow-nodes/settings/<Name>NodeSettings.tsx`). Chrome and widgets in `flow-nodes/`. Engine is consumed via `EngineContext` — neither component imports `DataflowEngine` directly.

> **Dual-component contract.** The graph card is **visual-only** — title, handles, live readouts, preview canvases. **No editable controls** as a default. Every parameter widget (slider, picker, file input, text field, point list) lives in the matching `*NodeSettings` component. The two surfaces are decoupled by design: authors tune the canvas card for at-a-glance legibility (compact, dense, focused on visual feedback) while the settings pane is dense with controls + extra readouts the right rail can afford. There is **no shared "headless" mode** — the legacy `<NodeControls>` wrapper + `useIsHeadlessNode` toggle are gone (the `HeadlessNodeContext` module survives only because `BaseNodeShell.HeaderTitle` still falls back through `useHeadlessNodeData` for inline rename; treat `useIsHeadlessNode` as effectively always-false in new code).
>
> **Documented exceptions to "visual-only" graph cards.** `EventEmitterNodeView` and `TapZoneNodeView` carry an inline EMIT button. Rationale: the most common authoring loop for both is "wire envelope/switch downstream → click EMIT → watch the pulse propagate". Sending authors into the right rail for every test would defeat the trigger UX. The `*NodeSettings` panes for these two processors keep the identity / throttle controls but **omit** the EMIT button — the canvas button is canonical, and showing two emit buttons in two places creates "which one fires?" confusion. If you need to add another emit-style trigger to the graph (a new event-source processor), follow the same split: button on the canvas, identity/throttle in settings, never both.
>
> **`FrameNodeView` is a third exception** but for a different reason: a frame is **not a processor** — it's a UI-only colored rectangle the author drops on the canvas to visually group nodes into "sections". Carries no input/output handles, no engine state, no edges; lives in `pages[].nodes[]` as `{ processor: 'frame', params: { color, label } }`. The view renders its own chrome (NOT `BaseNodeShell`): a thin draggable header strip with inline rename, plus a translucent body with `pointer-events: none` so clicks pass through to nodes laid on top of the frame. Frames are forced to `zIndex: -1` (per-node field + a `.react-flow__node-frame { z-index: -1 !important }` CSS rule) so they always paint behind processor cards even when selected. Spawning is via the toolbar **Frame** button only — frames don't appear in the Add-Node popover or the SceneOutlineSidebar (which lists processor originals, not markup). See `frame-constants.ts` for the palette + hex helpers; `frame-card.css` for chrome styling.

Scene is multi-page (tabs of independent scenarios) and supports UI-only **clones** (viewer aliases of any node anywhere). Engine sees a single flat dataflow — pages and clones resolved by `sceneResolver.resolveSceneForEngine` before reaching `engine.setEdges`.

