## NodeEditor

Three concerns:
1. **State** — `useSceneState()` (pages, activePageId, page CRUD, cross-page mutations), `engineRef`, `pin.state`, `fps`, `contextMenu`, `publishMessage`. The two rails (Outline, Settings) are **permanent surfaces** — no open/closed state, no localStorage keys. RF gets `activePage.nodes/edges`; `onNodesChange/onEdgesChange` route through `setActiveNodes/setActiveEdges`.
2. **Lifecycle** — Pixi `Application` init via `usePipelineEngine`, scene restore via `applySnapshot`, FPS push to engine. Single engine sync effect: `useEffect(() => engine.setEdges(resolveSceneForEngine(pages).resolvedEdges), [pagesStructuralHash, engineReady])`. Hash deliberately excludes viewport / page name / ordering so a tab switch doesn't cascade into `rebuildGraph` + "mark every node dirty" — that was the cause of the user-visible page-switch freeze.
3. **User actions** — connect (`onConnect` with one-edge-per-target-handle dedupe + `wouldCreateCycle` rejection), validate (`isValidConnection` resolves clones, consults `SLOT_COMPAT`, runs cycle check), add processor (`onAddFromPopover` autogenerates `id` for `tapZone` / `eventEmitter`), add clone (drag-and-drop from outline → `onCanvasDrop` → `addCloneNode`), delete (skip clones), copy/paste/undo/redo, export/import/clear, **Publish** (`derivePublishedSurface(pages)` → download `<effectId>.published.json` or surface first error in snackbar), pan/zoom (`onMoveEnd → pushSnapshot`).

`<ReactFlow>` wrapped in `<SceneProvider>` + `<EngineProvider>` + `<PinProvider>`. Body is a single horizontal row: `SceneOutlineSidebar | flow + tabs | RightRail`. Both rails are permanent (always mounted, no toggle button, no close button) — only their widths are user-controlled via edge-resize handles, persisted to `localStorage["nodeEditor.outlineSidebarWidth.v1"]` / `localStorage["nodeEditor.rightRail.width.v1"]`. RF does NOT remount on page swaps (`key={activePageId}` was removed — the remount cost was the user-visible "freeze on tab switch"). Loading gate shows "Initializing GPU..." until `engineReady`.

## Multi-page scenes & clones

- **Pages** — named tabs (`pages: PageState[]`, `activePageId`). Each owns its own `nodes/edges/viewport`. Switching saves the live viewport into the leaving page; rAF-restores incoming viewport (so RF has remounted before `setViewport`).
- **Clones** (`PipelineNodeData.cloneOf` set) — UI-only viewer aliases of an original node anywhere in the scene: no input handles, output handles derived from `PROCESSOR_CATALOG[origin.processor].def.outputs`, REF badge, dashed `.pn--clone` border. Multiple clones of the same original allowed; clones of clones not (outline excludes them; `findOriginAcrossPages` refuses to follow them).
- **Clone insertion** — drag-and-drop from `SceneOutlineSidebar` (left rail). Sidebar lists pages → originals; dragging sets `CLONE_DRAG_MIME = 'application/x-effect-clone-of'` (= origin id); canvas `onDrop` decodes + creates a clone via `addCloneNode`. Click navigates (`jumpToNode`); double-click activates host page without toggling collapse. `AddNodePopover` carries only fresh-processor items.
- **Engine sees only resolved scene.** Single sync effect `engine.setEdges(resolveSceneForEngine(pages).resolvedEdges)` whenever `pages` changes. `engine.addNode` / `engine.removeNode` happen in `onAddFromPopover` / `onNodesDelete` and skip clones unconditionally.
- **Cycle prevention** — `wouldCreateCycle(pages, source, target)` resolves both endpoints through clone aliases, BFSes forward over resolved adjacency, rejects if it would reach the candidate source. Same check in `isValidConnection` (UI feedback) and `onConnect` (defensive).
- **Pins surface — bottom of the right rail, always visible.** `RightRail` stacks two **permanent** surfaces vertically: `PublishInspector` (Settings) on top, `PinsPanel` (Pins) on the bottom. No tab switcher — both panels are always live and always reachable. A 4 px horizontal resize handle between them lets the user adjust the Pins panel height (persisted to `localStorage["nodeEditor.rightRail.pinsHeight.v1"]`, min 80 px; the Settings panel above keeps a 120 px floor). `PinsPanel` iterates `usePinning().pinnedIds`, finds each id in `useScene().pages` (cross-page scan), and mounts the registered `pipelineNodeTypes[data.processor]` component **headless** — wrapped in `HeadlessNodeProvider value={true}` + `NodeIdProvider value={id}` + `HeadlessNodeDataProvider value={data}`. Headless mode in `BaseNodeShell` already suppresses `<Handle>` ports + `NodeResizer`, so each pinned card shows the editor's actual `*NodeView` chrome (header, label, live preview / readouts, pin toggle) **without input/output points**. Caveats inherited from headless mode: pinned cards that read upstream wiring through React Flow's `useStore` (`EffectNodeView`, `AnimationControllerNodeView`) only see edges from the **active** page; engine outputs (`useNodeOutputs`) work cross-page because the engine sees the union scene.
- **Labels first-class.** `data.label` editable inline by double-clicking the header (graph mode only); empty clears back to `def.title`. CloneNodeView shows the live label of the original, never lets you edit it on the clone.
- **Broken clones don't cascade-delete.** When the original is deleted the clone keeps existing as a slate-coloured `.pn--clone--broken` placeholder. Edges from broken clones dropped by `resolveSceneForEngine` so the engine doesn't choke.

### `PipelineNodeData`

```ts
type PipelineNodeData = {
  processor: string
  params: Record<string, unknown>
  label?: string
  imageUrl?: string         // image processor only
  segPoints?: SamPoint[]
  segPolygon?: number[]
  segMaskKey?: string
}
```

Engine state lives in `DataflowEngine`; only persistence-relevant duplicates leak into `data`. Don't store transient runtime state in `data.params` — it'll be undo'd into staleness.

## BaseNodeShell

Renders `NodeResizer` (selected, grid-snapped), `.pn-header` (coloured top border via `--pn-accent` from `categoryColor(category)`), `.pn-handles` (input/output `<Handle>`s + labels), `.pn-widgets` (children), `PinToggle`.

### Grid alignment (don't break)

```
ROW_H = 20       # row pitch — must equal GRID_SIZE
ROW_TOP_PAD = 10 # top inset before first port
.pn-header height = 36px
.pn top border = 4px
```

First port centre lands at Y = 60 from the node's top edge — exactly 3 grid cells. Subsequent ports inherit alignment (`ROW_H = GRID_SIZE`). Any change to header height or `ROW_TOP_PAD` must keep first-port centre on a grid dot.

### Legacy headless mode (effectively dead — keep `BaseNodeShell` simple)

The old "headless" toggle on `BaseNodeShell` (skip handles + resizer when rendered off-canvas) is dead in practice — `*NodeSettings` components never invoke `BaseNodeShell` at all, and the pin terminal that previously hosted "headless" cards has been retired. `useIsHeadlessNode()` therefore always returns `false` in the live tree; the `headless` branch in `BaseNodeShell` survives only to keep the inline-rename fallback (`useHeadlessNodeData`) working without churn. Don't re-introduce conditional branches that depend on this toggle for new processors — author the graph card and the settings pane as fully independent components.

## EditableStepEdge

Custom orthogonal edge with draggable bend points. Bends persist in `edge.data.bends: { x, y }[]`. Hover shows midpoint handles per straight segment that drag perpendicular to the segment direction. When `bends` empty, falls back to RF's built-in smooth-step HVH path. Registered as `editableStep` in `node-editor/constants.ts → pipelineEdgeTypes` and applied via `defaultEdgeOptions`.

## Contexts (`flow-nodes/context/`)

- **`EngineContext`** — `EngineProvider value={engine}` + `useEngine()`.
- **`PinContext`** — `usePinning()` returns `{ isPinned, togglePin, pinnedIds, ... }`. Pin state captured in snapshots; lifted to `usePinState({ liveNodeIds })` above the provider. `liveNodeIds` is the union across pages — pins survive page switches and only get reaped on actual deletion.
- **`NodeIdContext`** — `useResolvedNodeId()` returns RF's `useNodeId` (graph) or override id. Same file exports `HeadlessNodeDataProvider` / `useHeadlessNodeData()`; retained for `BaseNodeShell.HeaderTitle`'s rename fallback. New code should not depend on this provider — `*NodeSettings` components receive `id` + `data` directly through `NodeSettingsProps`.
- **`HeadlessNodeContext`** — `useIsHeadlessNode()` → boolean. Effectively always `false` post-dual-component refactor; kept for `BaseNodeShell` compatibility only.

## SceneContext

```ts
{
    pages, activePageId, activePage,
    addPage(name?), removePage(id), renamePage(id, name), switchPage(id),
    allRealNodes(), findOrigin(cloneOfId),
    updateNodeData(id, partial),
    jumpToNode(nodeId),                  // switches page + centres on node
    setActiveNodes(updater), setActiveEdges(updater),
    replaceScene(pages, activePageId),
}
```

Lives inside `<ReactFlowProvider>` because `switchPage` / `jumpToNode` consult `useReactFlow().setViewport / setCenter`. Updates to `pages` trigger the `engine.setEdges` sync effect — single point at which the engine learns about UI changes.

## Hooks

- **`useSetParam(id)`** → `(key, value) => engine.updateNodeParams(id, { ...params, [key]: value })`.
- **`useSetNodeLabel(id)`** → `(label) => SceneContext.updateNodeData(id, { label })`. Empty clears to `def.title`. Powers inline rename in `BaseNodeShell.HeaderTitle`.
- **`useSetExposed(id)`** → `(exposed | undefined) => SceneContext.updateNodeData(id, { exposed })`. `undefined` clears. Used by `PublishInspector`.
- **`useSetRuntimeDynamic(id)`** → `(on: boolean) => SceneContext.updateNodeData(id, { runtimeDynamic: on ? true : undefined })`. Toggles the per-node "Compute live in player" flag consumed by the supplier-app bake step (`@effects/player → bakePipeline` taint seed). Surfaced by `PublishInspector`'s "Bake behaviour" section, only on bake-eligible processors (pure with a CONTOUR/TEXTURE output, or `segmentation`). See `player.mdc → Bake step`.
- **`useNodeOutputs(id)`** → `Record<string, unknown>`. Subscribes to `engine.subscribeNode`; initial state hydrated synchronously from `engine.getOutputs(id)` so views see latest values immediately on mount (without this, non-`alwaysDirty` processors like Config show stale data until the user touches an upstream param).
- **`useCanvasFill`** — Canvas2D sizing helper (DPR-aware).

## Widgets

All exported through `widgets/index.ts`: `Field`, `NumberField`, `TextFieldRow`, `SelectField`, `SwitchField`, `SliderField`, `ColorField` (when `FieldDef.color = true`), `ActionButton`, `StatusLine` (`normal | muted | error`), `SectionTitle`.

`Field` defaults to `layout='column'` — label above the control, control fills row width. Cleanest for inspector forms with mixed-length / localised labels (no truncation, uniform vertical rhythm). Pass `layout='row'` (or legacy `column={false}`) for compact two-column rows where vertical space is at a premium; row mode uses a fixed `LABEL_W = 130 px` label cell and ellipsis-truncates anything wider, so it's only suitable for short English labels.

Conventions:
- `slotProps={{ htmlInput: { className: 'nodrag' } }}` on every text input so dragging text doesn't pan the canvas.
- Styled via MUI `sx` and `node-card-mui.css` overrides scoped to `.pn`.
- Spacing unit 6 px, buttons 28 px tall, inputs 24 px, dense everywhere.

## Per-processor components — graph view + settings pane

Each processor ships **two independent files** registered in two parallel registries:

- `views/<Name>NodeView.tsx` → `pipelineNodeTypes` (consumed by React Flow as the canvas card).
- `settings/<Name>NodeSettings.tsx` → `pipelineNodeSettings` (consumed by `PublishInspector`).

### `*NodeView` — graph canvas card

Visual-only. Wraps `BaseNodeShell` for chrome (header + handles + REF badge + inline rename + pin toggle). Its `children` slot is for **read-only visual feedback**: live readouts, oscilloscope canvases, profile previews, status lines, image previews. **Never** an editable widget.

```tsx
export const FooNodeView = memo(({ data }: NodeProps & { data: PipelineNodeData }) => {
    const value = (data.params.x ?? 0) as number
    return (
        <BaseNodeShell title={fooDef.title} category={fooDef.category}
                       inputs={fooDef.inputs} outputs={fooDef.outputs}>
            <StatusLine tone="muted">x: {value}</StatusLine>
        </BaseNodeShell>
    )
})
```

### `*NodeSettings` — settings pane

All editable controls. Receives `{ id, data }: NodeSettingsProps`; calls `useSetParam(id)` to update params. Renders **without** `BaseNodeShell` — `PublishInspector`'s `SectionBlock` provides the panel-side framing (top divider + uppercase heading + breathing room). Free to render whatever layout suits the right rail (dense grids, sections, custom labelling).

```tsx
export const FooNodeSettings = memo(({ id, data }: NodeSettingsProps) => {
    const set = useSetParam(id)
    return (
        <Field label="Param">
            <NumberField value={data.params.x} onChange={v => set('x', v)} />
        </Field>
    )
})
```

A processor without editable params has **no** `*NodeSettings` entry (e.g. `effect`, `clone`, `contourPreview`, `textStrip`, `sdfFromContour`); the panel falls back to "no editable settings".

### Cross-cutting

Connection hints (`StatusLine` like "wire X to Y") belong on **consumer** graph cards only. Input nodes (Text, TextStyle, TextStrip) carry no hints.

Some views read the upstream Config for effect-specific labels (`EffectNodeView` for texture slots; `AnimationControllerNodeView` for animation slots):

```ts
const upstreamId = useStore(useMemo(() => (s: ReactFlowState) =>
  s.edges.find(e => e.target === nodeId && e.targetHandle === 'config')?.source ?? '', [nodeId]))
const upstreamOutputs = useNodeOutputs(upstreamId)
```

Re-renders both when the wire changes (selector against `s.edges`) and when the upstream Config publishes a new effect name. The same hook is reused inside `*NodeSettings` (e.g. `AnimationControllerNodeSettings` reuses `useUpstreamSlots`) — the runtime context is identical, both files sit inside the same `<ReactFlowProvider>` + `<EngineProvider>`.

### Adding a new processor (UI side)

1. Create `views/<Name>NodeView.tsx` — visual-only card.
2. Create `settings/<Name>NodeSettings.tsx` — editable controls (skip if the processor has none).
3. Register in `nodeTypes.ts → pipelineNodeTypes`.
4. Register in `settingsTypes.ts → pipelineNodeSettings` (skip if no settings).
5. Add to `node-editor/menuGroups.ts → RAW_GROUPS`.

## Right rail (`RightRail`)

Permanent right-side surface. Stacks two always-visible panels vertically — `PublishInspector` (Settings, top, flex-grow) and `PinsPanel` (Pins, bottom, fixed-but-resizable). Each carries its own 32 px section header (`Settings` / `Pins`) so users know which surface they're looking at; both render flush against the rail (no close affordance, no tab strip). The user controls three things, all persisted:
- Rail width — 4 px left-edge resize handle, `localStorage["nodeEditor.rightRail.width.v1"]`, clamped 280..720, default 360.
- Pins panel height — 4 px horizontal resize handle between Settings and Pins, `localStorage["nodeEditor.rightRail.pinsHeight.v1"]`, min 80 px, default 280. The Settings panel above keeps a 120 px floor.

Pinned cards in the Pins panel are mounted continuously (no lazy / hidden fallback) so engine subscriptions inside them (`PreviewNodeView`'s `engine.subscribeNode`, `LogNodeView`'s metric polling, etc.) keep ticking even while the user is editing settings.

## Publish Inspector (Settings panel)

**Right rail, top half.** Permanent surface; renders flush against the rail (no close affordance, no internal header — the rail's `Settings` section header above the panel labels it). Body is a vertical stack of `SectionBlock`s — each block carries an uppercase heading + a top divider so the panel reads as a sequence of distinct concerns instead of one undifferentiated scroll. The first block (per-node title group: label + `processor · id`) carries no top divider so it sits flush with the panel's top edge. Padding is owned by `SectionBlock` (`px: 2; pt: 2; pb: 2`, `gap: 1`) rather than the body, so dividers run edge-to-edge across the full panel width and headings get breathing room above and below the divider line.

Responsibilities, rendered in this order as separate `SectionBlock`s:

1. **Per-node title group** — node label (or `def.title`), `processor · nodeId` caption.
2. **`Parameters`** — resolves `pipelineNodeSettings[node.data.processor]` and renders it inline (no card frame; the section block already provides padding + divider). Falls back to "This node has no editable parameters" for processors without a registered settings component (pure visualisers `effect`, `clone`, `contourPreview`, `textStrip`, `sdfFromContour`). **Skipped entirely** when the processor only carries actions (`pipelineNodeActions[proc]` set, `pipelineNodeSettings[proc]` unset — currently `preview`); otherwise the empty-state hint would print "no editable parameters" right above an `Actions` block that clearly contradicts it.
3. **`Actions`** — resolves `pipelineNodeActions[node.data.processor]` and renders it inline. Reserved for **export-style side-effects** that don't mutate scene state: file downloads (Save image), clipboard writes, snapshot exports, optionally "reset to defaults" pulses. Section omitted when no entry is registered. Same `NodeSettingsProps` contract as Settings, same engine-context plumbing.
4. **`Publish summary`** (only when the selected node is `publishRoot`) — live `derivePublishedSurface(pages)` results: image-slot count, tap-zone list, exposed fields, whole nodes, graph trim sizes, validation errors.
5. **`Supplier exposure`** (every processor except `publishRoot`) — Tier-2 `data.exposed` controls written via `useSetExposed`:
   - `image` / `segmentation` / `tapZone` / `eventEmitter` / other irreducible-UI processors: **"Expose to supplier" toggle** (`mode='whole'`), with optional `label` + `hint` overrides.
   - `config`: per-`FieldDef` checkboxes for the active effect (`mode='fields'` with `fields: string[]`).
   - `clone`: read-only hint — clones aren't part of the published surface (the original is).

**Don't add new content directly under the body Box** — wrap it in a `SectionBlock` with an explicit heading, otherwise the visual rhythm of "heading + divider + content" breaks. If you need a tightly-coupled cluster (e.g. two related sub-toggles), nest them inside a single `SectionBlock`; keep section count low so the panel stays scannable.

## Toolbar

Buttons: back link, FPS dropdown, undo/redo, **Frame** (spawns a UI-only colored markup rectangle at the viewport centre — see `FrameNodeView`), **Publish** (downloads `<effectId>.published.json` via `derivePublishedSurface`; first error → snackbar), export/import/clear. FPS dropdown lists `FPS_OPTIONS` (`[0, 5, 10, 15, 30, 60]`); persisted to `localStorage["nodeEditor.fps.v1"]`. `0` = uncapped.

The toolbar carries **no rail-toggle buttons** — Outline (left rail) and Settings (right rail) are permanent workspace surfaces. Don't reintroduce show/hide toggles for either; the rule is "always visible, only width is user-controlled".

## Theme

Custom dark MUI theme: Geist/Vercel-inspired palette, JetBrains Mono Variable 700 (Inter Variable fallback), 6 px spacing unit, 28 px buttons, 24 px inputs, dense switches/checkboxes/sliders. `@fontsource` packages ship variable fonts; loaded in `main.tsx`. No Tailwind, no shadcn, no CSS-in-JS beyond MUI `sx`.

## CSS conventions

- Plain `.css` files in `flow-nodes/styles/` (not modules), class-prefixed (`.pn`, `.pn-header`, `.pn-body`, `.pn-handles`, `.pn-widgets`, `.pn-resize-line`, `.pn-resize-handle`, `.pn-edge-*`).
- `node-card.css` — chrome, header, handles, widgets, resize handles.
- `node-card-mui.css` — MUI overrides scoped under `.pn`.
- `editable-edge.css` — orthogonal edge + bend-points.
- `pin-terminal.css` — pin-terminal cards.

Don't add CSS modules, Emotion `styled`, or Tailwind. The dense `.pn` scoping is intentional and makes per-processor view CSS unnecessary.

## Pitfalls

- Importing `DataflowEngine` directly in a view or settings pane → breaks engine/UI separation. Use `useEngine()`.
- Putting an editable widget inside `*NodeView` → graph card MUST stay visual-only (with the documented EMIT-button exceptions on `EventEmitterNodeView` / `TapZoneNodeView`). The right home is `*NodeSettings`. Duplicating the widget on the canvas costs maintenance with zero gain.
- Adding a new processor without registering in BOTH `pipelineNodeTypes` and `pipelineNodeSettings` → graph card renders but the Settings panel falls back to "no editable settings" (or vice versa). Skip the settings registration only when the processor genuinely has no params.
- Re-introducing `<NodeControls>` or `useIsHeadlessNode()`-based conditional rendering → the dual-component refactor explicitly removed this pattern; use independent `*NodeView` and `*NodeSettings` files instead.
- Storing transient state in `data.params` → persisted, undone, restored. Keep in component state or processor instance fields.
- Missing `nodrag` on a text input → canvas pans while typing.
- New array reference from `useNodeOutputs` consumer's `useMemo` without proper deps → unnecessary re-renders. Prefer `useNodeOutputs(id).foo` directly when only one field is needed.
- Hard-coding `ROW_H` in a view → drifts from `GRID_SIZE` and edges fan off-grid. Always use `BaseNodeShell` for chrome; if you need a fixed-pixel layout, snap to `GRID_SIZE`.
- Not setting `minWidth` on a wide visual area in `*NodeView` → resizer's default min (160) too small for some cards (preview, log, signal-switch oscilloscope). Pass `minWidth={...}`. `*NodeSettings` doesn't need `minWidth` — the right rail owns its width.
- Forgetting `memo` on a view or settings pane → RF (or PublishInspector) re-renders on every interaction.
- Reintroducing a "show/hide rail" toolbar button → both rails are permanent. If a future need calls for a transient utility panel, add it as a third surface (modal, overlay, popover) rather than putting Outline / Settings behind a toggle again.
- `Typography sx={{ color: 'text.primary' }}` inside a `*NodeView` (graph card) → MUI's `text.primary` resolves to the dark-theme white token (`geist.gray900 ≈ #ededed`), which is invisible on the light card body (`--pn-bg ≈ #d4d4d4`). Use `color: 'var(--pn-text, #0a0a0a)'` (or `var(--pn-text-secondary)` / `var(--pn-text-muted)`) so the text picks up the dark token scoped to `.pn`. The `*NodeSettings` panes don't have this problem — they live on the dark right-rail surface where MUI's `text.primary` is correct.
- Forcing a fixed `height` on `MuiInputBase.sizeSmall` in the theme without a `&.MuiInputBase-multiline { height: auto }` carve-out → multiline `TextField rows={N}` collapses to 28 px and the inner `<textarea>` overflows above its wrapper, painting on top of the field label. The harmonised input height applies to single-row controls only.
- Putting an export-style button (Save image, Download mask, Copy as JSON) inside `*NodeSettings` → the inspector renders it under the `Parameters` heading, which misreads as "this is a tunable knob". Register it in `pipelineNodeActions` instead (`*NodeActions` component) so it lands under the `Actions` SectionBlock. If the same processor has no params, its `pipelineNodeSettings` entry should be omitted — the inspector then skips the `Parameters` block entirely rather than printing "no editable parameters" above the action button.
- `engine.addNode` for a clone → phantom processor. Only `onAddFromPopover({ kind: 'processor', ... })` and `applySnapshot`'s non-clone branch may invoke `engine.addNode`. Copy/paste preserves `cloneOf`.
- Operating on `nodes`/`edges` instead of `pages` in editor-level effects → only the active page is observed; changes on inactive pages get missed. Use `pages` (or `useScene().allRealNodes()`).
